If a class is outside these families, use dedicated plotting helpers
or custom \code{ggplot2} graphics on component tables.

For \code{mfrm_category_curves}, pass \code{preset = "monochrome"} for
grayscale/line-type output. Cumulative \code{.5} boundary lines are shown
only for interpretable in-range boundaries by default; use
\code{boundary_status = "all"} to show every finite boundary estimate or
\code{boundary_status = "none"} / \code{show_cumulative_boundaries = FALSE} to
suppress those vertical boundary lines. Use
\code{plot_data(x, component = "plot_long")} on a category-curve bundle when
you want one ggplot2/plotly-friendly table across all curve families.
Keep curve families and groups separate in custom figures. All category-curve,
category-structure, rating-scale, and network bundle views render natively
via \code{ggplot2}. Step displays use point estimates and do not turn stored step
SEs into crossing, gap or curve intervals.

\code{summary()} method: Summarize a summary-table bundle for manuscript QC

For \code{summary()}: Review returned-table coverage, dimensions, roles
and numeric content before plotting or export.

\code{plot()} method: Plot a summary-table bundle for manuscript QC

For \code{plot()}: Table-count views describe reporting coverage, not
study sample sizes. \verb{selection_*} views use appendix selections already
retained in the bundle; \code{"selection_handoff_role_sections"} groups them by
reporting role and appendix section. Numeric views pass the selected table
through \code{\link[=apa_table]{apa_table()}} and \code{\link[=plot.apa_table]{plot.apa_table()}} for exploratory checks. See
\code{type} for each view and \code{selection_value} for counts versus fractions.
}
\section{Interpreting output}{

\itemize{
\item \code{overview}: class, component count, and selected preview component.
\item \code{summary}: one-row aggregate block when supplied by the bundle.
\item \code{preview}: first \code{top_n} rows from the main table-like component.
\item \code{settings}: resolved option values if available.
\item \code{validation_scope}: package-generated versus independently supplied
evidence scope when
summarizing \code{mfrm_reference_benchmark}.
\item \code{conquest_command_scope}: ConQuest command-template scope when summarizing
\code{mfrm_conquest_overlap_bundle}.
\item \code{conquest_output_contract}: requested ConQuest outputs and review handoff
when summarizing \code{mfrm_conquest_overlap_bundle}.
\item \code{mfrmr_fit_status}: actual optimizer controls, MML engine, convergence
evidence, and inference-readiness state for an \code{mfrm_conquest_overlap_bundle}.
\item \code{normalization_scope}: extracted-table normalization scope when summarizing
\code{mfrm_conquest_overlap_tables}.
\item \code{review_scope}: supplied-table review scope when summarizing
\code{mfrm_conquest_overlap_review}.
\item \code{conquest_overlap_checks} / \code{population_policy_checks}: specialized
benchmark check previews when summarizing \code{mfrm_reference_benchmark}.
}


The returned object is a \code{ggplot} object whose underlying plot payload and
notes remain accessible via \code{\link[=plot_data]{plot_data()}} and \code{\link[=plot_data_components]{plot_data_components()}}; set
\code{draw = TRUE} for immediate display.


For \code{summary()}:
\itemize{
\item \code{overview}: source class, returned-table count, note count, and whether a
numeric table is available for plotting.
\item \code{role_summary}: counts and total size by reporting role.
\item \code{table_catalog}: complete returned-table registry with plot/export bridges.
\item \code{table_profile}: table-level dimensions, numeric-column counts, and missing
values for the largest returned tables.
\item \code{plot_index}: which returned tables are plot-ready and which bundle-level
numeric QC routes they support.
\item \code{appendix_presets}: conservative \code{all} / \code{recommended} / \code{compact}
plus section-aware \code{methods} / \code{results} / \code{diagnostics} / \code{reporting}
appendix-export presets derived from table roles.
\item \code{appendix_role_summary}: counts of returned tables by reporting role under
the same conservative appendix routing used by the bundle catalog.
\item \code{appendix_section_summary}: counts of returned tables by manuscript-facing
appendix section.
\item When workflow appendix tables are present, \code{selection_handoff_table_summary},
\code{selection_handoff_preset_summary}, \code{selection_handoff_bundle_summary},
\code{selection_handoff_role_summary} and \code{selection_handoff_role_section_summary}
describe export selections by table, preset, bundle-by-section, reporting role
and role-by-section, respectively.
\item \code{selection_summary}, \code{selection_table_summary},
\code{selection_table_preset_summary}, \code{selection_role_summary},
\code{selection_section_summary} and \code{selection_catalog} describe the
preset-filtered selections from those workflow tables.
\item \code{reporting_map}: where to go next for plotting, APA formatting, and export.
\item \code{notes}: carried forward source-level caveats from the originating summary.
}
}

\section{Typical workflow}{

\enumerate{
\item Generate a bundle table/report helper output.
\item Run \code{summary(bundle)} for compact QA.
\item Drill into specific components via \code{$} and visualize with \code{plot(bundle, ...)}.
}


\enumerate{
\item Create bundle output (e.g., \code{unexpected_response_table()}).
\item Inspect routing with \code{summary(bundle)} if needed.
\item Call \code{p <- plot(bundle, type = ..., draw = FALSE)} and inspect \code{plot_data(p)} when needed.
}
}

\section{Session plot defaults}{

See \link{mfrmr_visual_diagnostics} for \code{options(mfrmr.plot_preset)}, argument
precedence, and appearance when saving or reusing a plot.
}

\section{Network display controls}{

For subset, design, rater and halo bundles with \code{type = "network"}, use
\code{layout} (a two-column coordinate matrix, optionally row-named by all node IDs,
or a function of the displayed igraph), \code{display_min_weight} (absolute edge
weight for the picture only), and \code{graphical} for supported compatibility
controls: \code{vertex.label} and the first positive \code{vertex.size} value. Other
igraph styling controls are not applied by the ggplot renderer; customize
the returned ggplot instead.
Unnamed vectors and coordinates follow the returned \code{nodes} order. All nodes,
including isolates, remain. The default layout is a deterministic circle; positions
do not estimate anything. Labels are hidden by default above 80 nodes;
set vertex.label explicitly to override. Supply the same named layout to compare views.
Constructor filters such as \code{min_weight} change calculated metrics;
\code{display_min_weight} does not. Returned \code{source_edges} retain the calculated
graph, \code{edges} the displayed graph, and \code{coordinates} the actual positions.
Titles are metadata by default; add displayed text with \code{ggplot2::labs()}.
See \href{https://ryuya-dot-com.github.io/mfrmr/dev/articles/mfrmr-networks-and-reporting.html}{online networks and reporting guide} for bipartite assignment,
directional disagreement and rater-by-criterion examples inspired by Lamprianou.
With \code{\link[=rater_network_analysis]{rater_network_analysis()}} and \code{weight_metric = "DirectionalMagnitude"},
\verb{type = "fit", metric = "SWS"} (or \code{"PUS"}) shows the published network
inconsistency index. The returned table retains unavailable rows/statuses;
there are no calibrated reference cutoffs. An entirely unavailable index
draws an unavailable message and retains the original rows/statuses in the
returned table, rather than substituting zero. For signed alpha,
\code{type = "matrix"} returns all eligible signed pair coefficients before graph filtering, retaining
zero and undefined values separately. Network drawings use magnitude for
edge width and dashed lines for negative agreement. Use
\code{\link[=plot.mfrm_network_ergm]{plot.mfrm_network_ergm()}} for ERGM coefficient intervals and simulated GOF.
}

